<section class="section lab-section" id="projects" aria-labelledby="projects-heading"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / The homelab</p><h2 id="projects-heading">Curiosity, put into practice.</h2></div><p>Real infrastructure, real changes, and the troubleshooting and recovery decisions behind them.</p></div>
<article class="project featured-project" id="ai-sysadmin" aria-labelledby="ai-heading"><span class="index">01</span><div><p class="project-label">Featured / AI &amp; infrastructure</p><h3 id="ai-heading">AI sysadmin: operating Gaia with scoped access</h3><p>I built an AI sysadmin workflow with scoped permissions across every Proxmox server in my homelab. It has grown from diagnostic assistance into a practical way to carry out complex infrastructure work under my direction.</p><div class="tags"><span class="tag">AI agents</span><span class="tag">Scoped permissions</span><span class="tag">Proxmox VE 9</span><span class="tag">Proxmox Backup Server</span></div><div class="project-takeaway"><h4>My role</h4><p>I set the goals and permission boundaries, directed the work with AI, and planned recovery throughout. The project brings together systems administration, troubleshooting, and decisions about what an agent should be allowed to do.</p></div></div><div class="project-detail"><h4>What I've delivered</h4><ul class="outcomes"><li><strong>Proxmox 8 → 9 cluster upgrade.</strong> Used AI exclusively to carry out the upgrade under my direction.</li><li><strong>Dedicated backup infrastructure.</strong> Set up Proxmox Backup Server together with AI as part of the lab's recovery approach.</li><li><strong>GPU-backed Ollama maintained.</strong> Worked through significant host, NVIDIA, and LXC hurdles to maintain GPU access for local inference.</li><li><strong>A recovery path throughout.</strong> Planned emergency console access using a NanoKVM USB as a portable crash cart.</li></ul><h4>What this demonstrates</h4><p>Applying AI to consequential technical work, defining access boundaries, diagnosing failures across system layers, and keeping recovery in view during major changes.</p><details><summary>The engineering behind the result</summary><p>The hardest work sits between layers: the Proxmox host, NVIDIA drivers and userspace libraries, container device access, and Ollama's ability to use the GPU. Troubleshooting has included host/container library mismatches and stale NVIDIA device permissions that prevented CUDA offload even when nvidia-smi could see the GPU.</p><p>The recovery plan includes a NanoKVM USB for direct screen and keyboard access from a laptop when ordinary remote administration is unavailable. It is a planned emergency route; the backup server is already set up.</p></details></div></article>
<article class="project"><span class="index">02</span><div><h3>Gaia: a self-hosted ecosystem</h3><p>A multi-node homelab bringing together compute, storage, identity, remote access, and services I use at home.</p><div class="tags"><span class="tag">Proxmox</span><span class="tag">Linux / LXC</span><span class="tag">Docker</span><span class="tag">ZFS</span></div></div><div class="project-detail"><h4>What I work with</h4><p>Distributing services across hardware with different capabilities; running containers and virtual machines; connecting local services through Traefik and remote access through a VPS.</p><h4>What this demonstrates</h4><p>Systems integration, Linux administration, networking, and practical decisions about where a service belongs.</p><details><summary>Inside the project</summary><p>The lab combines a NAS, an Intel NUC, a Dell system, and a GPU-equipped laptop. Authentik provides identity for supported services. Prometheus, Grafana, and Uptime Kuma provide monitoring, while Cloudflare DNS and certificate automation support service access.</p></details></div></article>
<article class="project"><span class="index">03</span><div><h3>Local AI on Olympus</h3><p>Using an existing GPU laptop as a host for local language models, with Ollama running inside a Linux container.</p><div class="tags"><span class="tag">Ollama</span><span class="tag">NVIDIA / CUDA</span><span class="tag">LXC</span><span class="tag">Open WebUI</span></div></div><div class="project-detail"><h4>What I work with</h4><p>GPU access inside a container, Linux driver compatibility, and the relationship between available hardware and local model choices.</p><h4>What this demonstrates</h4><p>Diagnosing issues across hardware, the host operating system, and the container environment.</p><details><summary>Inside the project</summary><p>Olympus has an RTX 3080 Mobile GPU and 64 GB of system memory. The Ollama container uses NVIDIA libraries and CUDA, with troubleshooting extending from GPU visibility to CUDA offload and actual model acceleration. Maintaining this workload has become a key part of my AI sysadmin project.</p></details></div></article>
<article class="project"><span class="index">04</span><div><h3>A home that responds</h3><p>Connecting physical controls, lighting, and software through Home Assistant, with an emphasis on useful behavior.</p><div class="tags"><span class="tag">Home Assistant</span><span class="tag">Zigbee / ZHA</span><span class="tag">Inovelli</span><span class="tag">Automation</span></div></div><div class="project-detail"><h4>What I work with</h4><p>Smart-bulb mode, switch events, and lighting scenes that connect a familiar wall control to configurable behavior.</p><h4>What this demonstrates</h4><p>Translating a household need into an interaction, then troubleshooting the device and software layers that make it work.</p><details><summary>Inside the project</summary><p>Inovelli switches and bulbs are connected through ZHA. The lighting work includes scene control and attention to the transition between scenes, including the goal of activating a red-light scene directly without a flash of the previous setting.</p></details></div></article>
<article class="project"><span class="index">05</span><div><h3>One identity across services</h3><p>Connecting supported self-hosted applications to Authentik, including a working OpenID Connect integration with Gitea and Google sign-in.</p><div class="tags"><span class="tag">Authentik</span><span class="tag">OpenID Connect</span><span class="tag">Gitea</span><span class="tag">Identity &amp; access</span></div></div><div class="project-detail"><h4>What I built</h4><p>A shared identity layer for supported services, with groups organized around the people who use the lab and the access they need.</p><h4>What this demonstrates</h4><p>Integrating application authentication, understanding the relationship between sign-in and authorization, and making access decisions across a self-hosted environment.</p><details><summary>Inside the project</summary><p>Authentik connects Google sign-in to supported applications. I configured Gitea to use OpenID Connect and organized identity groups for family, friends, administrators, and different service roles.</p></details></div></article>
</section></main>
<section class="approach" id="approach" aria-labelledby="skills-heading"><div class="wrap approach-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / Practical experience</p><h2 id="skills-heading">Connecting the parts.<br>Understanding the whole.</h2></div><div><p>At work, I investigate data and integrations directly and translate business needs into delivery context. In the lab, I own the permissions, dependencies, and recovery path. Each strengthens the way I work with technical teams.</p><div class="capabilities"><div><h3>Systems & networking</h3><p>Linux, Proxmox VE, Proxmox Backup Server, containers, GPU access, storage, DNS, and remote access.</p></div><div><h3>Identity & observability</h3><p>Authentik, OIDC integrations, Prometheus, Grafana, and uptime monitoring.</p></div><div><h3>AI & automation</h3><p>Scoped agent access, AI-assisted cluster upgrades, Ollama, Open WebUI, and n8n workflows.</p></div><div><h3>Data & product tools</h3><p>SQL Server, Postman, advanced Excel/VBA, story definition, prioritization, and Jira backlog management.</p></div></div></div></div></section>
<section class="section wrap" id="about" aria-labelledby="about-heading"><div class="about-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">04 / How I got here</p><h2 id="about-heading">I learned the operation.<br />Then started improving it.</h2><p>My path into product began in policy operations and underwriting support. Building tools to solve the problems in front of me led to IT, business systems analysis, and company-wide digital product management.</p><p>That background still shapes my approach: learn how the work actually happens, identify the friction, and connect the people and systems needed to improve it. Outside work, the homelab keeps that learning practical.</p></div><div class="career"><h3>Digital Product Manager</h3><p class="company">McNeil &amp; Co. · Selected for the product role in 2020</p><p>I work across underwriting, claims, finance, HR, and risk management, shaping stories and priorities with business leadership and IT. My leadership comes through facilitation, technical credibility, and a clear understanding of the operation.</p><p style="margin-top:18px">I lead the AI user group, present at IT town halls, and work directly with developers, analysts, and vendors to turn product intent into workable decisions.</p></div></div>
<div class="career-journey"><h3>A career built from the inside out</h3><ol class="journey-grid"><li><span>01 / Operations</span><strong>Policy Analyst</strong><p>Policy issuance, forms, coverage, and underwriting data.</p></li><li><span>02 / Underwriting</span><strong>Rater / Underwriting Assistant</strong><p>Rating, endorsements, proposals, and the pricing calculator.</p></li><li><span>03 / Business systems</span><strong>Business Analyst</strong><p>Core-system expertise, database logic, integration, and workflow automation.</p></li><li><span>04 / Product</span><strong>Digital Product Manager</strong><p>Company-wide planning, modernization, stakeholder alignment, and applied AI.</p></li></ol></div>
